{"abstract":"Objective: To describe the implementation of whole genome sequencing (WGS) in prenatal diagnostics and outline the national guideline system facilitating this. Methods: Clinical guidelines for WGS in prenatal diagnostics were developed and implemented by the Danish Fetal Medicine Society. Results: Guidelines were developed by expert consensus following a review of 75 studies. Diagnostic yield served as a key factor in prioritizing WGS for various phenotypes, improving diagnostic accuracy and informing clinical decisions. Phenotypes for WGS include nuchal translucency ≥ 6.0 mm, multiple anomalies, skeletal dysplasia, neuromuscular diseases, non-immune hydrops fetalis, central nervous system malformations, congenital diaphragmatic hernia and severe fetal growth restriction (< 3 SDs not explained by placental insufficiency). Small regional variations exist in indications, bioinformatics, and funding, but WGS is now routinely used nationwide for these indications. Conclusion: The Danish Fetal Medicine Society's guideline development, emphasizing diagnostic yield and gradual implementation, has supported the relatively uniform integration of WGS into prenatal diagnostics.","author_count":18,"contributors":["Vogel, Ida","Andreasen, Lotte","Balslev-Harder, Marie","Becher, Naja","Ernst, Anja","Gadsbøll, Kasper","Hjortshøj, Tina Duelund","Hvidbjerg, Marie Skov","Larsen, Martin","Lou, Stina","Lund, Ida Charlotte Bay","Pedersen, Lars Henning","Sønderberg Roos, Laura Kirstine","Sperling, Lene","Sunde, Lone","Tørring, Pernille Mathiesen","Vedel, Cathrine","Petersen, Olav Bjørn"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-813330"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Vogel, Ida"],"keyword":["Clinical guidelines","Denmark","Diagnostic yield","Genomic medicine","Prenatal diagnostics","Public healthcare system","Whole genome sequencing","prenatal screening and diagnostic technologies"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"whole genome sequencing in prenatal diagnostics the danish approach to guideline formation and implementation within public healthcare","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/pd.6780","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-813330","identifier":[{"id":"40122702","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105000738948","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-3b9d7e49-c813-47e7-91f7-af0ff4f6f265","au-4c1f872a-86b1-4377-969d-138332e62e91"],"keyword":["Denmark","clinical guidelines","diagnostic yield","genomic medicine","prenatal diagnostics","prenatal screening and diagnostic technologies","public healthcare system","whole genome sequencing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B8EB6B55254377D696357D9E8481454D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/pd.6780","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region",