Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Yangfan","key":"liupau","last":"Liu","locid":"f126113a-b3c0-4506-b43a-9dcf113b924e","name":"Liu, Yangfan","orcid":"0000-0003-0812-8853","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Jung","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"b93ddd14-b340-4550-b3cf-eb9473b0d716","name":"Kjær, Lene Jung","orcid":"0000-0001-9659-4672","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anette Ella","key":"boklundpau","last":"Boklund","locid":"b56cee2e-0320-4c3d-a360-6dc1f5b2ee67","name":"Boklund, Anette","name_org":"Boklund, Anette Ella","orcid":"0000-0001-6359-3403","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Charlotte Kristiane","key":"hjulsagerpau","last":"Hjulsager","name":"Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars Erik","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"135a9407-d29c-4508-8f79-978f5b295a15","name":"Larsen, Lars Erik","orcid":"0000-0003-0730-5555","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten Thure","key":"kirkebypau","last":"Kirkeby","locid":"ea24c9a4-7aa6-40f3-9afb-0af19f1e962d","name":"Kirkeby, Carsten","name_org":"Kirkeby, Carsten Thure","orcid":"0000-0001-9292-5526","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38450025"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fvets.2024.1358995","issn":["22971769"],"journal":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","paperid":"1358995","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85186576005"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8690b168-f312-4901-847b-28cb58208cfd"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Risk factors for avian influenza in Danish poultry and wild birds during the epidemic from June 2020 to May 2021","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-731700","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Umbilical outpouchings (UO) in pigs present a welfare concern because of ulceration risk and complications. Danish legislation requires pigs with larger UOs to be housed in sick pens with soft bedding, and some UO pigs might not be suited for transport. Because of this, many UO pigs are euthanized, adding to the costs of pig production. The true prevalence of UO is unknown as no scientific reports with randomly sampled herds exist. This study aimed to estimate the prevalence of UO in Danish piglets and weaners and describe their clinical characteristics: size, texture, reducibility, and occurrence of ulcers. Lastly, risk factors for the occurrence of ulcers on UOs were investigated. Results: A cross-sectional study was conducted in 30 Danish conventional herds, with at least 800 weaned pigs and 200 sows. The herds were selected randomly from the Danish Husbandry Register and visited once between September 2020 and May 2021. Piglets were examined during their last week in the farrowing unit, and weaners were examined between weeks three and eight after weaning. The abdominal area was palpated on all pigs, and all irregularities were recorded; the results presented are umbilical outpouchings measuring at least 2 × 2 cm. The within-herd prevalence of piglets with UO averaged 4.2% with a range from 0.8 to 13.6% between herds. The within-herd prevalence of weaners with UO averaged 2.9%, ranging from 1.0 to 5.3% between herds. Approximately 80% of the UOs were classified as small or medium (< 7 cm piglets/ < 11cm weaners). Large outpouchings had significantly higher odds of ulcer occurrence (OR = 9.9, p < 0.001). Conclusion: UOs are common in Denmark, with a prevalence of 2.9% in weaners and an estimated annual production of 32 million Danish pigs almost a million pigs are affected yearly. Most of these pigs will have a small or medium UO. If the pigs have large UOs the odds of ulcer occurrence increase significantly. Numerous of these pigs are wasted, challenging sustainability and economy. UOs might also affect the welfare of the pigs. More research is therefore needed, especially in the prevention of UOs.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hansen, Marie Louise","Jensen, Tina Birk","Kristensen, Charlotte Sonne","Larsen, Inge","Pedersen, Ken Steen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-731703"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Hernia","Pig","Piglet","Sustainability","Umbilical outpouching","Weaner"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-SEGES"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-SEGES","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"umbilical outpouchings in danish piglets and weaners prevalence and clinical characteristics a cross sectional study at herd level","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40813-023-00352-8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-731703","identifier":[{"id":"85182145658","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38218966","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10788040","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-86fac566-f3bf-4465-9728-de913556fb3c"],"keyword":["Hernia","Pig","Piglet","Sustainability","Umbilical outpouching","Weaner"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4DFCD9C9A8EC1C1CEC5FF7022C002DF5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40813-023-00352-8","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/385518615/s40813_023_00352_8.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Production, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"seges innovation","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-SEGES","l3nn":"SEGES Innovation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SEGES Innovation","namevar":["SEGES Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-SEGES","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Ø-Vet A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Marie Louise","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"d4e731e0-3535-4e5b-92b3-857d21d575fc","name":"Hansen, Marie Louise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Tina Birk","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Tina Birk","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Charlotte Sonne","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","name":"Kristensen, Charlotte Sonne","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Inge","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"82b09bf9-73d5-4c71-a3c1-01e66b0e9ebe","name":"Larsen, Inge","orcid":"0000-0003-4575-2241","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Ken Steen","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"e7e2a10f-e368-4635-9481-f23e58c80aa8","name":"Pedersen, Ken Steen","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38218966"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s40813-023-00352-8","issn":["20555660"],"journal":"Porcine Health Management","paperid":"3","status":"p","title":"Porcine Health Management","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85182145658"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","ø-vet a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-86fac566-f3bf-4465-9728-de913556fb3c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Umbilical outpouchings in Danish piglets and weaners : prevalence and clinical characteristics - a cross-sectional study at herd level","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-731703","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Adverse Childhood Experiences (ACEs) have been identified as a major public health challenge in Greenland. No previous studies have created a multi-item ACE- scale among an Arctic Indigenous population.  Objective: To develop a multi-item ACE-scale among Inuit youth in Greenland (the ACEIG scale).  Methods: The ACEIG scale was created with data from the ‘Wellbeing among Inuit youth in Greenland’-survey. Scale items were based on a recognised ACE-scale and further adapted to the context of Inuit youth in Greenland by adding items relevant for the population. The scale was validated through item response theory (IRT) and reliability was assessed by Cronbach's alpha.  Results: Four items relevant for Inuit youth in Greenland were added to the recognised ACE scale (bullying, death of parent, gambling problems in close family, and suicide in close relations). The scale was reduced by IRT, as three items (bullying, divorce of parents and parents passing away) exceeded the difficulty index threshold. The ACEIG scale therefore consists of 10 items: alcohol problems in close family, marijuana use in close family, domestic violence, being victim of physical violence, being victim of psychological violence, any type of sexual abuse, sexual abuse (intercourse), sexual abuse (more than once), suicide in close relations, and gambling problems in close family. Cronbach's alpha was 0.7.  Conclusion: The ACEIG scale includes 10 items with acceptable reliability. The scale can inform future screening tools to identify vulnerable youth and target interventions. Future studies should investigate the association between the ACEIG scale and health outcomes.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Ottendahl, Charlotte Brandstrup","Sørensen, Ivalu","Beck, Astrid","Pedersen, Cecilia Petrine","Bjerregaard, Peter","Larsen, Christina Viskum Lytken"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-734184"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Nordic"],"corresponding_author":["Ottendahl, Charlotte Brandstrup"],"country":["Greenland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Grønlands Universitet"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adverse childhood experiences","Greenland","Greenland epidemiology","Humans","Inuit","Item response theory","Reproducibility of Results","Surveys and Questionnaires","Youth"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"developing the aceig scale an adverse childhood experience scale for inuit youth in greenland","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chiabu.2023.106471","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We would like to thank all participants who gave their time to be involved in the survey. Financial support was received from the department of family, culture, church and gender equality in Greenland and the National Institute of Public Health in Denmark."],"id":"S01-000.00-734184","identifier":[{"id":"85173225906","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37821291","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-bf194882-2744-4c46-8693-77b7cb03712b"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adverse childhood experiences","Greenland","Greenland/epidemiology","Humans","Inuit","Item response theory","Reproducibility of Results","Surveys and Questionnaires","Youth"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CB37247F20AF26DB28525AB0ED29481B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chiabu.2023.106471","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/255819712/1-s2.0-S0145213423004593-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Center for Public health in Greenland"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gl","key":"grønlands universitet","loc":["Greenland","Nordic"],"name":"Grønlands Universitet","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte Brandstrup","key":"ottendahlpau","last":"Ottendahl","locid":"9e2ae085-ebf8-4688-b8cf-c257c5458aca","name":"Ottendahl, Charlotte Brandstrup","orcid":"0000-0002-9285-3099","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ivalu Katajavaara","key":"seidlerpau","last":"Seidler","locid":"5e19e227-e860-49db-af28-67b29e038c9c","name":"Sørensen, Ivalu","name_org":"Seidler, Ivalu Katajavaara","orcid":"0000-0002-4956-6326","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Astrid","key":"beckpau","last":"Beck","name":"Beck, Astrid","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Cecilia Petrine","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Cecilia Petrine","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"bjerregaardpau","last":"Bjerregaard","locid":"ef4a2fa7-9735-4b1e-a4fa-b1272def2cfc","name":"Bjerregaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-7153-8447","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina Viskum Lytken","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"3ee738b1-09d1-4886-9ddf-67ca132997fe","name":"Larsen, Christina Viskum Lytken","orcid":"0000-0002-6245-4222","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37821291"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.chiabu.2023.106471","issn":["01452134","18737757"],"issn_org":["01452134"],"journal":"Child Abuse & Neglect","paperid":"106471","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Child Abuse & Neglect","volume":"148"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85173225906"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-bf194882-2744-4c46-8693-77b7cb03712b"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Developing the ACEIG-scale : An adverse childhood experience scale for Inuit youth in Greenland","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-734184","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To investigate if the effect of cardiac computed tomography (CT) vs. invasive coronary angiography (ICA) on cardiovascular events differs based on smoking status. MATERIALS AND METHODS: This pre-specified subgroup analysis of the pragmatic, prospective, multicentre, randomised DISCHARGE trial (NCT02400229) involved 3561 patients with suspected coronary artery disease (CAD). The primary endpoint was major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE: cardiovascular death, non-fatal myocardial infarction, or stroke). Secondary endpoints included an expanded MACE composite (MACE, transient ischaemic attack, or major procedure-related complications). RESULTS: Of 3445 randomised patients with smoking data (mean age 59.1 years + / - 9.7, 1151 men), at 3.5-year follow-up, the effect of CT vs. ICA on MACE was consistent across smoking groups (p for interaction = 0.98). The percutaneous coronary intervention rate was significantly lower with a CT-first strategy in smokers and former smokers (p = 0.01 for both). A CT-first strategy reduced the hazard of major procedure-related complications (HR: 0.21, 95% CI: 0.03, 0.81; p = 0.045) across smoking groups. In current smokers, the expanded MACE composite was lower in the CT- compared to the ICA-first strategy (2.3% (8) vs 6.0% (18), HR: 0.38; 95% CI: 0.17, 0.88). The rate of non-obstructive CAD was significantly higher in all three smoking groups in the CT-first strategy. CONCLUSION: For patients with stable chest pain referred for ICA, the clinical outcomes of CT were consistent across smoking status. The CT-first approach led to a higher detection rate of non-obstructive CAD and fewer major procedure-related complications in smokers. CLINICAL RELEVANCE STATEMENT: This pre-specified sub-analysis of the DISCHARGE trial confirms that a CT-first strategy in patients with stable chest pain referred for invasive coronary angiography with an intermediate pre-test probability of coronary artery disease is as effective as and safer than invasive coronary angiography, irrespective of smoking status. TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov NCT02400229. KEY POINTS: • No randomised studies have assessed smoking status on CT effectiveness in symptomatic patients referred for invasive coronary angiography. • A CT-first strategy results in comparable adverse events, fewer complications, and increased coronary artery disease detection, irrespective of smoking status. • A CT-first strategy is safe and effective for stable chest pain patients with intermediate pre-test probability for CAD, including never smokers.","author_count":83,"contributors":["Mancone, Massimo","Mézquita, Aldo J Vázquez","Birtolo, Lucia Ilaria","Maurovich-Horvat, Pal","Kofoed, Klaus Fuglsang","Benedek, Theodora","Donnelly, Patrick","Rodriguez-Palomares, Jose","Erglis, Andrejs","Štěchovský, Cyril","Šakalytė, Gintare","Ađić, Nada Čemerlić","Gutberlet, Matthias","Diez, Ignacio","Davis, Gershan","Zimmermann, Elke","Kępka, Cezary","Vidakovic, Radosav","Francone, Marco","Ilnicka-Suckiel, Małgorzata","Plank, Fabian","Knuuti, Juhanni","Faria, Rita","Schröder, Stephen","Berry, Colin","Saba, Luca","Ruzsics, Balazs","Rieckmann, Nina","Kubiak, Christine","Hansen, Kristian Schultz","Müller-Nordhorn, Jaqueline","Merkely, Bela","Sigvardsen, Per E","Benedek, Imre","Orr, Clare","Valente, Filipa Xavier","Zvaigzne, Ligita","Suchánek, Vojtěch","Jankauskas, Antanas","Ađić, Filip","Woinke, Michael","Keane, Stephen","Lecumberri, Ignacio","Thwaite, Erica","Kruk, Mariusz","Jovanovic, Vladimir","Kuśmierz, Donata","Feuchtner, Gudren","Pietilä, Mikko","Ribeiro, Vasco Gama","Drosch, Tanja","Delles, Christian","Palmisano, Vitanio","Fisher, Michael","Drobni, Zsófia D","Kragelund, Charlotte","Aurelian, Rosca","Kelly, Stephanie","Del Blanco, Bruno Garcia","Rubio, Ainhoa","Boussoussou, Melinda","Hove, Jens Dahlgaard","Rodean, Ioana","Regan, Susan","Calabria, Hug Cuéllar","Becker, Dávid","Larsen, Linnea","Hodas, Roxana","Napp, Adriane E","Haase, Robert"