University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Green Societies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Malene Charlotte","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"cc0033d3-6794-4e0c-b451-939e05f9a1ee","name":"Larsen, Malene Charlotte","orcid":"0000-0003-4821-5855","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["1903301X"],"journal":"Videnskab.dk","status":"p","title":"Videnskab.dk","title_org":"videnskab.dk"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-4e0bd02d-47ae-48e7-bbc3-0b3faec22469"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"»Er de to sammen lige nu?« Snap Map kan skabe ufrivillig overvågning","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-756703","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Women with a family history of breast and/or ovarian cancer have an increased ovarian cancer risk. Yet it remains uncertain if common ovarian cancer risk factors-especially those that are modifiable-affect this high-risk population similarly to the general population. METHODS: Using the Danish and Swedish nationwide registers, we established 2 nested case-control study populations in women with a family history of breast and/or ovarian cancer (2138 ovarian cancers, 85 240 controls) and women without (10 730 ovarian cancers, 429 200 controls). The overall and histology-specific associations were assessed with conditional logistic regression. The country-specific estimates were combined based on a fixed-effect assumption. RESULTS: Multiparity, hysterectomy, tubal ligation, salpingectomy, and oral contraceptive (OC) use were associated with a reduced risk of ovarian cancer in women with and without a family history, while endometriosis and menopausal hormone therapy were associated with increased risk. Multiparity and OC use presented protective effects across all histologic subtypes except mucinous ovarian cancer, which was not associated with OC use. Menopausal hormone treatment increased the risk of serous ovarian cancer but decreased the risk of the mucinous and clear cell cancers. Endometriosis was especially related to an increased risk of endometrioid and clear cell ovarian cancer. CONCLUSION: Factors associated with a decreased ovarian cancer risk were similar between women with and without a family history of breast and/or ovarian cancer. Given the higher baseline risk for women with a family history, special attention should be paid to risk factors like endometriosis and nulliparity in this high-risk population.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Zheng, Guoqiao","Baandrup, Louise","Wang, Jiangrong","Hertzum-Larsen, Rasmus","Hannibal, Charlotte Gerd","Faber, Mette Tuxen","Sundström, Karin","Kjaer, Susanne K."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-777964"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institutet","Karolinska University Hospital"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ovarian cancer risk factors in relation to family history","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-777964","identifier":[{"id":"85208771637","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38964345","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-694d750a-9a3d-4c3e-8159-b687ad34835f"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E0459A99B23FBB9A3681B66980540335"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Danish Cancer Society"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital - Roskilde"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"se","key":"karolinska university hospital","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska University Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Guoqiao","key":"zhengpau","last":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guoqiao","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Louise","key":"baandruppau","last":"Baandrup","locid":"abccf449-8bed-4f98-bb4d-012134523360","name":"Baandrup, Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Jiangrong","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiangrong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Rasmus","key":"hertzumlarsenpau","last":"Hertzum-Larsen","name":"Hertzum-Larsen, Rasmus","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Charlotte Gerd","key":"hannibalpau","last":"Hannibal","name":"Hannibal, Charlotte Gerd","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mette Tuxen","key":"faberpau","last":"Faber","name":"Faber, Mette Tuxen","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","first":"Karin","key":"sundströmpau","last":"Sundström","name":"Sundström, Karin","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 6","first":"Susanne K.","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"1c671dbc-6a27-49fd-ba11-6d26a9a07047","name":"Kjaer, Susanne K.","name_org":"Kjær, Susanne K.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38964345"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/jnci/djae164","issn":["00278874","14602105"],"issn_org":["00278874"],"issue":"11","journal":"Journal of the National Cancer Institute","pages":"1767-1774","status":"p","title":"Journal of the National Cancer Institute","volume":"116"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85208771637"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-694d750a-9a3d-4c3e-8159-b687ad34835f"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Ovarian cancer risk factors in relation to family history","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-777964","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Spinal manipulation (SM) has been claimed to change anatomy, either in structure or position, and that these changes may be the cause of clinical improvements. The aim of this systematic review was to evaluate and synthesise the peer-reviewed literature on the current evidence of anatomical changes in response to SM. Methods: The review was registered with PROSPERO (CRD42022304971) and reporting was guided by the standards of the PRISMA Statement. We searched Medline, Embase, CINAHL, AMED, Cochrane Library all databases, PEDro, and the Index to Chiropractic Literature from inception to 11 March 2022 and updated on 06 June 2023. Search terms included manipulation, adjustment, chiropractic, osteopathy, spine and spine-related structures. We included primary research studies that compared outcomes with and without SM regardless of study design. Manipulation was defined as high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust delivered by hand to the spine or directly related joints. Included studies objectively measured a potential change in an anatomical structure or in position. We developed a novel list of methodological quality items in addition to a short, customized list of risk of bias (RoB) items. We used quality and RoB items together to determine whether an article was credible or not credible. We sought differences in outcomes between SM and control groups for randomised controlled trials and crossover studies, and between pre- and post-SM outcomes for other study designs. We reported, in narrative form, whether there was a change or not. Results: The search retrieved 19,572 articles and 20 of those were included for review. Study topics included vertebral position (n = 3) facet joint space (n = 5), spinal stiffness (n = 3), resting muscle thickness (n = 6), intervertebral disc pressure (n = 1), myofascial hysteresis (n = 1), and further damage to already damaged arteries (n = 1). Eight articles were considered credible. The credible articles indicated that lumbar facet joint space increased and spinal stiffness decreased but that the resting muscle thickness did not change. Conclusion: We found few studies on this topic. However, there are two promising areas for future study: facet joint space and spinal stiffness. A research strategy should be developed with funding for high quality research centres.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Young, Kenneth J.","Leboeuf-Yde, Charlotte","Gorrell, Lindsay","Bergström, Cecilia","Evans, David W.","Axén, Iben","Chance-Larsen, Kenneth","Gagey, Olivier","Georgopoulos, Vasileios","Goncalves, Guillaume","Harris, Catherine","Harsted, Steen","Kerry, Roger","Lee, Edward","McCarthy, Christopher","Nim, Casper","Nyirö, Luana","Schweinhardt, Petra","Vogel, Steven"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-783278"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Young, Kenneth J."],"country":["France","Norway","South Africa","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Balgrist University Hospital","Et Liv i Bevegelse (Norwegian Chiropractic Research Foundation)","Karolinska Institute","Manchester Metropolitan University","NIHR Applied Research Collaboration North West Coast","Umeå University","University College of Osteopathy","University of Birmingham","University of Central Lancashire","University of Johannesburg","University of Nottingham","University of Paris-Saclay","Western Norway University of Applied Sciences"],"keyword":["Chiropractic","Humans","Manipulation Spinal methods","Mechanism","Osteopathy","Physiotherapy","Spinal manipulation","Systematic review"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mechanisms of manipulation a systematic rev