LL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-e28d227b-9a3d-470a-9b7c-c4188f778814","ku-d2bab5d3-8753-467e-b93e-f2f2c9384fbc","sdu-109ef03c-a13b-413b-8ac0-3df4fc69ef1a"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Pericardial delta like non-canonical NOTCH ligand 1 (Dlk1) augments fibrosis in the heart through epithelial to mesenchymal transition","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-100.00-735043","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Breast cancer survivors are a growing population due to improved treatment. It is known that postmenopausal women treated for breast cancer may experience weight gain and increased insulin resistance, but detailed knowledge on how chemotherapy impact metabolic and endocrine mechanisms remain unknown. Objectives: We performed a thorough, preliminary study to elucidate the differing mechanisms of postprandial absorption and metabolism in postmenopausal early breast cancer (EBC) patients treated with adjuvant chemotherapy compared to healthy controls. We hypothesize that chemotherapy has a negative impact on metabolism in EBC patients. Methods: We examined four postmenopausal women shortly after treatment with chemotherapy for EBC and four age-matched healthy women who served as controls using isotopic tracers during a mixed meal-test. Blood was sampled during the 240 min meal-test to examine postprandial absorption and endogenous synthesis of lipid and carbohydrate metabolites. Results: We found that insulin concentrations were numerically higher before the meal-test in the EBC patients compared to controls (76.3 pmol/L vs 37.0 pmol/L; P = 0.06). Glucose kinetics was increased postprandial (most pronounced at 30 min, 9.46 mmol/L vs 7.33 mmol/L; P = 0.51), with no difference between the groups regarding liver glucose output. Fatty acid kinetics showed a numeric increase in oleic acid rate of appearance in BC patients, but only during the first hour after the mixed meal. There was no significant difference in VLDL-TAG synthesis between the two groups. Conclusions: This preliminary study is unique in using advanced tracer methods to investigate in vivo metabolism of EBC patients after chemotherapy although no statistical differences in glucose and fatty acid kinetics was seen compared to controls. However, during the first two postprandial hours, oral glucose and oleic acid appearance in the systematic circulation was elevated in the EBC patients. This could be due to changes in gastrointestinal uptake and further studies with altered set-up could provide valuable insights.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Buch-Larsen, Kristian","Gillberg, Linn","Ahmed, Haboon Ismail","Marstrand, Simone Diedrichsen","Andersson, Michael","van Hall, Gerrit","Brøns, Charlotte","Schwarz, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-731590"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Breast cancer","Chylomicron","Fatty acids","Glucose","Meal test","Metabolism"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"postabsorptive and postprandial glucose and fat metabolism in postmenopausal women with breast cancer preliminary data after chemotherapy compared to healthy controls","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nut.2024.112394","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-731590","identifier":[{"id":"38458062","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85187377252","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-7c3a0901-3e93-4604-9d35-92bcfd3b8a1f"],"keyword":["Breast cancer","Chylomicron","Fatty acids","Glucose","Meal test","Metabolism","VLDL-TAG"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7A27B6DBD1F73F459AE18B7F11D6DE0B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nut.2024.112394","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/385692332/1_s2.0_S0899900724000443_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Exercise and Muscle"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Kristian","key":"buchlarsenpau","last":"Buch-Larsen","name":"Buch-Larsen, Kristian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Linn","key":"gillbergpau","last":"Gillberg","locid":"55b1a9fd-b331-4c69-8a4e-4e851766fb05","name":"Gillberg, Linn","orcid":"0000-0003-0588-1663","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Haboon Ismail","key":"ahmedpau","last":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Haboon Ismail","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Simone Diedrichsen","key":"marstrandpau","last":"Marstrand","name":"Marstrand, Simone Diedrichsen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"anderssonpau","last":"Andersson","name":"Andersson, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Gerrit","key":"vanhallpau","last":"van Hall","locid":"55a9b111-6dbb-4a42-a9ab-d5f3785fbdae","name":"van Hall, Gerrit","orcid":"0000-0002-2519-8127","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Charlotte","key":"brønspau","last":"Brøns","name":"Brøns, Charlotte","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Peter","key":"schwarzpau","last":"Schwarz","locid":"058b1228-5dc0-458a-a98c-3faded28305b","name":"Schwarz, Peter","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38458062"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.nut.2024.112394","issn":["08999007","18731244"],"issn_org":["08999007"],"journal":"Nutrition","paperid":"112394","status":"p","title":"Nutrition","volume":"122"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85187377252"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7c3a0901-3e93-4604-9d35-92bcfd3b8a1f"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Postabsorptive and postprandial glucose and fat metabolism in postmenopausal women with breast cancer—Preliminary data after chemotherapy compared to healthy controls","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-731590","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"During the UK 2020-2021 epizootic of H5Nx clade 2.3.4.4b high-pathogenicity avian influenza viruses (HPAIVs), high mortality occurred during incursions in commercially farmed common pheasants (Phasianus colchicus). Two pheasant farms, affected separately by H5N8 and H5N1 subtypes, included adjacently housed red-legged partridges (Alectoris rufa), which appeared to be unaffected. Despite extensive ongoing epizootics, H5Nx HPAIV partridge outbreaks were not reported during 2020-2021 and 2021-2022 in the UK, so it is postulated that partridges are more resistant to HPAIV infection than other gamebirds. To assess this, pathogenesis and both intra- and inter-species transmission of UK pheasant-origin H5N8-2021 and H5N1-2021 HPAIVs were investigated. Onward transmission to chickens was also assessed to better understand the risk of spread from gamebirds to other commercial poultry sectors. A lower infectious dose was required to infect pheasants with H5N8-2021 compared to H5N1-2021. However, HPAIV systemic dissemination to multiple organs within pheasants was more rapid following infection with H5N1-2021 than H5N8-2021, with the former attaining generally higher viral RNA levels in tissues. Intraspecies transmission to contact pheasants was successful for both viruses and associated with viral environmental contamination, while interspecies transmission to a first chicken-contact group was also efficient. However, further onward transmission to additional chicken contacts was only achieved with H5N1-2021. Intra-partridge transmission was only successful when high-dose H5N1-2021 was administered, while partridges inoculated with H5N8-2021 failed to shed and transmit, although extensive tissue tropism was observed for both viruses. Mortalities among infected partridges featured a longer incubation period compared to that in pheasants, for both viruses. Therefore, the susceptibility of different gamebird species and pathogenicity outcomes to the ongoing H5Nx clade 2.3.4.4b HPAIVs varies, but pheasants represent a greater likelihood of H5Nx HPAIV introduction into galliforme poultry settings. Consequently, viral maintenance within gamebird populations and risks to poultry species warrant enhanced investigation.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Seekings, Amanda H.","Liang, Yuan","Warren, Caroline J.","Hjulsager, Charlotte K.","Thomas, Saumya S.","Lean, Fabian Z.X.","Nunez, Alejandro","Skinner, Paul","Selden, David","Falchieri, Marco","Simmons, Hugh","Brown, Ian H.","Larsen, Lars Erik","Banyard, Ashley C.","Slomka, Marek J."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-739545"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The Royal Veterinary College","UK Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs"],"keyword":["H5N1"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"transmission dynamics and pathogenesis differ between pheasants and partridges infected with clade 000000000002 000000000003 000000000004 000000000004b h000000000005n000000000008 and h000000000005n000000000001 high pathogenicity avian influenza viruses","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1099/jgv.0.001946","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-739545","identifier":[{"id":"85184344421","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38289661","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-9b2fd4f1-02cc-4203-b18b-0ec4a52402d0"],"keyword":["H5N1","H5N8","clade 2.3.4.4b","high-pathogenicity avian influenza viruses (HPAIVs)","partridges","pheasants"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-25A47F9E62EF9173A71A058E10FA1224"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1099/jgv.0.001946","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/389666884/jgv001946.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"uk department for environment food and rural affairs","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"UK Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Veterinary Clinical Microbiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"the royal veterinary college","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The Royal Veterinary College","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Amanda H.","key":"seekingspau","last":"Seekings","name":"Seekings, Amanda H.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Yuan","key":"liangpau","last":"Liang","locid":"15fec07b-8b15-410c-9db2-b2b7fda77736","name":"Liang, Yuan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Caroline J.","key":"warrenpau","last":"Warren","name":"Warren, Caroline J.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Charlotte K.","key":"hjulsagerpau","last":"Hjulsager","name":"Hjulsager, Charlotte K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Saumya S.","key":"thomaspau","last":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Saumya S.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","first":"Fabian Z.X.","key":"leanpau","last":"Lean","name":"Lean, Fabian Z.X.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Alejandro","key":"nunezpau","last":"Nunez","name":"Nunez, Alejandro","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Paul","key":"skinnerpau","last":"Skinner","name":"Skinner, Paul","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"David","key":"seldenpau","last":"Selden","name":"Selden, David","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marco","key":"falchieripau","last":"Falchieri","name":"Falchieri, Marco","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hugh","key":"simmonspau","last":"Simmons","name":"Simmons, Hugh","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ian H.","key":"brownpau","last":"Brown","name":"Brown, Ian H.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lars E.","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"135a9407-d29c-4508-8f79-978f5b295a15","name":"Larsen, Lars Erik","name_org":"Larsen, Lars E.","orcid":"0000-0003-0730-5555","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ashley C.","key":"banyardpau","last":"Banyard","name":"Banyard, Ashley C.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marek J.","key":"slomkapau","last":"Slomka","name":"Slomka, Marek J.","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38289661"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1099/jgv.0.001946","issn":["00221317","14652099"],"issn_org":["00221317"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of General Virology","paperid":"001946","status":"p","title":"Journal of General Virology","title_org":"The Journal of general virology","volume":"105"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184344421"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9b2fd4f1-02cc-4203-b18b-0ec4a52402d0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Transmission dynamics and pathogenesis differ between pheasants and partridges infected with clade 2.3.4.4b H5N8 and H5N1 high-pathogenicity avian influenza viruses","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-739545","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION AND AIM: Radiotherapy in cancer patients with a pacemaker or an implantable cardioverter defibrillator might damage the device and possibly result in malfunction of the device. We aimed to examine device malfunction and change in lead parameters in this group. METHODS: The medical records of pacemaker/implantable cardioverter defibrillator patients who underwent radiotherapy at Aalborg University Hospital between July 1, 2016, and June 30, 2021, were reviewed. Treatment was planned according to risk of device malfunction, assessed using local guidelines. Bootstrap linear regression was used to identify possible differences in lead parameters depending on beam energy and anatomical location. RESULTS: One hundred nine patients with a pacemaker/implantable cardioverter defibrillator who received a total of 122 radiotherapy courses in the study period were identified. No device malfunctions or significant changes in lead parameters during radiotherapy were observed. Only the left ventricle lead threshold and impedance were found to be significantly different when compared by beam energy; however, these changes were relatively small and unlikely to be of clinical relevance. CONCLUSION: No device malfunction or clinically relevant changes in lead parameters were identified in this study, suggesting that radiotherapy of pacemaker/implantable cardioverter defibrillator patients can be regarded as safe when following relevant safety precautions.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Larsen, Mia Bang","Jakobsen, Annette Charlotte","Lundbye-Christensen, Søren","Riahi, Sam","Thøgersen, Anna Margrethe","Zaremba, Tomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-752341"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Larsen, Mia Bang"],"keyword":["Implantable cardioverter defibrillator","Ionizing radiation","Pacemaker","Radiotherapy"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluation of cardiac implantable electronic device lead parameters before and after radiotherapy","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/pace.15048","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-752341","identifier":[{"id":"85199973254","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39078373","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-a65e7900-a26d-47d6-a804-4087b5e1030d"],"keyword":["Pacemaker","beam energy","device malfunctions","implantable cardioverter defibrillator","ionizing radiation","radiotherapy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-72D616A3947EE922DA54F86C6DA85C07"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/754333693/Larsen_et_al._2024_._Evaluation_of_cardiac_implantable_electronic_device_lead_parameters_before_and_after_radiotherapy.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/pace.15048","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mia Bang","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"e01ea21a-c165-469c-89f2-1434f6d05e27","name":"Larsen, Mia Bang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Annette Ross","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"d23643c9-77c0-4eb1-9932-be3b08a06e6c","name":"Jakobsen, Annette Charlotte","name_org":"Jakobsen, Annette Ross","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren","key":"lundbyechristensenpau","last":"Lundbye-Christensen","locid":"d2639b0b-0785-456c-8c8d-57e9e79e0569","name":"Lundbye-Christensen, Søren","orcid":"0000-0002-9420-2783","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sam","key":"riahipau","last":"Riahi","locid":"9e5489d8-981a-469c-bd84-20b2e24832de","name":"Riahi, Sam","orcid":"0000-0003-1849-9463","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna Margrethe","key":"thøgersenpau","last":"Thøgersen","locid":"040c49e1-e9f1-4e74-8cd0-4a9ef9fb7006","name":"Thøgersen, Anna Margrethe","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tomas","key":"zarembapau","last":"Zaremba","locid":"e328e721-f3d1-4d11-ba49-3b3194f2562d","name":"Zaremba, Tomas","orcid":"0000-0001-6365-6638","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39078373"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/pace.15048","issn":["01478389","15408159"],"issn_org":["01478389"],"issue":"9","journal":"Pacing and Clinical Electrophysiology","pages":"1176-1184","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Pacing and Clinical Electrophysiology","volume":"47"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85199973254"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-a65e7900-a26d-47d6-a804-4087b5e1030d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Evaluation of cardiac implantable electronic device lead parameters before and after radiotherapy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-752341","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: As healthcare systems evolve, individuals are expected to be more involved in managing their health and rehabilitation. A wireless medical accelerometer (SENS motion®) has been developed to collect objective data on physical activity. The number of patients requiring knee replacement is rising, but the motivational effect of medical accelerometers in the rehabilitation after knee replacement remains unexplored. This study aims to employ a user-driven approach to tailor the SENS motion® technology for patients undergoing knee replacement prior to testing the refined technology in a randomised controlled trial. Methods: The study used a Participatory Design research methodology, emphasising collaboration and user involvement. It was carried out in three sessions, each aimed at refining the SENS motion® system toward the needs of the patient group in focus. The first session involved six healthcare professionals who provided written feed-back. The second and third sessions included testing and subsequent interviews of patients (n = 10). After each session, conducted in iterative processes (plan, act, observe, reflect), SENS motion® system revisions were implemented according to the patient's wishes. The data collected were then analysed using qualitative content analysis. Results: Prior to patient testing, healthcare professionals identified functional and technical errors that required modifications. Patient interviews revealed that (1) there were positive attitudes towards the SENS motion® system, (2) patients were motivated by daily step counts and geographical locations, especially when they were familiar with landmarks, and (3) active involvement of family members was found to be feasible, which contributed to a sense of solidarity during the rehabilitation process. Conclusion: This study applied a user-driven approach to customise health technology for postoperative rehabilitation in knee replacement patients. Initially, the technology had both technical and functional problems, but system revisions based on patient feedback improved its acceptance. The refined technology is undergoing testing in a randomised design.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Skov, Cecilie Dollerup","Holsgaard-Larsen, Anders","Wiil, Uffe Kock","Lindberg-Larsen, Martin","Varnum, Claus","Myhre Jensen, Charlotte"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-760946"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Skov, Cecilie Dollerup"],"keyword":["Iterations","Knee replacement","Participatory Design","Patients experience","Qualitative Research","User involvement"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the patient s perspective on rehabilitation with wireless accelerometers activity tracking and motivational feedback following knee replacement a qualitative study prior to a randomised controlled trial kneeactivity","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2024.105624","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-760946","identifier":[{"id":"85203443126","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39265242","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-545999dd-1151-464a-ab05-6266e14786d4"],"keyword":["Iterations","Knee replacement","Participatory Design","Patients experience","Qualitative research","User involvement"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CD726FB3C5B0DB5C91B8EE3E1ADDE485"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2024.105624","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/271522932/1-s2.0-S1386505624002879-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of orthopaedic surgery (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cecilie D.","key":"skovpau","last":"Skov","locid":"62ddb8a5-b21c-4ec6-8fcc-3923a7a1db0c","name":"Skov, Cecilie Dollerup","name_org":"Skov, Cecilie D.","orcid":"0000-0002-6063-7113","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"holsgaardlarsenpau","last":"Holsgaard-Larsen","locid":"3c1cf5a4-81b1-49f3-ad79-d086b74b1561","name":"Holsgaard-Larsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-4106-6930","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Uffe","key":"kockwiilpau","last":"Kock Wiil","locid":"3c3acba0-f147-4923-8d16-48599c4bf797","name":"Wiil, Uffe Kock","name_org":"Kock Wiil, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0001-6898-4083","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"lindberglarsenpau","last":"Lindberg-Larsen","locid":"299608f9-db43-4744-984b-a7c428aca95b","name":"Lindberg-Larsen, Martin","orcid":"0000-0002-4483-677X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"varnumpau","last":"Varnum","locid":"0f639544-4ebc-467c-a15b-3c049a26f62f","name":"Varnum, Claus","orcid":"0000-0002-0625-5691","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte M.","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"a084c1c8-fe75-45d5-93f8-3c37b71b732b","name":"Myhre Jensen, Charlotte","name_org":"Jensen, Charlotte M.","orcid":"0000-0002-7058-4641","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39265242"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2024.105624","issn":["13865056","18728243"],"issn_org":["13865056"],"journal":"International Journal of Medical Informatics","paperid":"105624","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Medical Informatics","volume":"192"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85203443126"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-545999dd-1151-464a-ab05-6266e14786d4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The patient's perspective on rehabilitation with wireless accelerometers, activity tracking and motivational feedback following knee replacement : A qualitative study prior to a randomised controlled trial (KneeActivity)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-760946","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Vulvar lichen sclerosus (VLS) is a chronic inflammatory mucocutaneous disease known to be associated with human papillomavirus-independent vulvar squamous cell carcinoma. Evidence on the association with other types of cancer, however, is sparce. We conducted a large nationwide cohort study examining the incidence of non-vulvar cancers among women with biopsy-verified VLS compared with the general female population. By using the nationwide Pathology Registry, we identified all women in Denmark with a biopsy-verified VLS diagnosis during 1978–2019 (n = 16,921). The cohort was followed up in the Danish Cancer Registry until 2022 for a subsequent non-vulvar cancer diagnosis. Standardized incidence ratios (SIRs) were computed with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) as relative risk estimates of all specific non-vulvar cancer sites. Compared with general female population rates, women with biopsy-verified VLS had decreased rates of several non-vulvar cancers, including HPV-related cancers (combined estimate: SIR = 0.5; 95% CI: 0.3–0.7), and lung (SIR = 0.6; 95% CI: 0.5–0.7), liver (SIR = 0.5; 95% CI: 0.2–0.9), and thyroid cancer (SIR = 0.5; 95% CI: 0.3–0.9). The decreased SIRs tended to sustain throughout the follow-up period following the VLS diagnosis. This large nationwide cohort study shows that women with biopsy-verified VLS may have a long-term reduced risk of developing HPV-related (cervical, vaginal, oropharyngeal, and anal) and smoking-associated cancers (lung, liver, and cervical) as well as thyroid cancer. Future studies focusing on the mechanisms behind the decreased cancer risk are needed.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Emma L. Kaderly","Hannibal, Charlotte Gerd","Hertzum-Larsen, Rasmus","Kjaer, Susanne K.","Baandrup, Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764586"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cancer risk","Human papillomavirus","Vulvar Lichen Sclerosus"],"l