</sub> storage","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-371557","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Avian influenza (AI) is a contagious disease of birds with zoonotic potential. AI virus (AIV) can infect most bird species, but clinical signs and mortality vary. Assessing the distribution and factors affecting AI presence can direct targeted surveillance to areas at risk of disease outbreaks, or help identify disease hotspots or areas with inadequate surveillance. Using virus surveillance data from passive and active AIV wild bird surveillance, 2006-2020, we investigated the association between the presence of AIV and a range of landscape factors and game bird release. Furthermore, we assessed potential bias in the passive AIV surveillance data submitted by the public, via factors related to public accessibility. Lastly, we tested the AIV data for possible hot- and cold spots within Denmark. The passive surveillance data was biased regarding accessibility to areas (distance to roads, cities and coast) compared to random locations within Denmark. For both the passive and active AIV surveillance data, we found significant (p < .01) associations with variables related to coast, wetlands and cities, but not game bird release. We used these variables to predict the risk of AIV presence throughout Denmark, and found high-risk areas concentrated along the coast and fjords. For both passive and active surveillance data, low-risk clusters were mainly seen in Jutland and northern Zealand, whereas high-risk clusters were found in Jutland, Zealand, Funen and the southern Isles such as Lolland and Falster. Our results suggest that landscape affects AIV presence, as coastal areas and wetlands attract waterfowl and migrating birds and therefore might increase the potential for AIV transmission. Our findings have enabled us to create risk maps of AIV presence in wild birds and pinpoint high-risk clusters within Denmark. This will aid targeted surveillance efforts within Denmark and potentially aid in planning the location of future poultry farms.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Kjær, Lene Jung","Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","Larsen, Lars Erik","Boklund, Anette","Halasa, Tariq","Ward, Michael P","Kirkeby, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-445857"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Sydney"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"landscape effects and spatial patterns of avian influenza virus in danish wild birds 000000002006 000000002020","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/tbed.14040","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-445857","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9291307","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85127140646","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33600073","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-57a79472-73dc-45b0-9f09-40d23ff4bb4e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-407B9434F5E7875647F713A587195FD4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/308556699/Transbounding_Emerging_Dis_2021_Kj_r_Landscape_effects_and_spatial_patterns_of_avian_influenza_virus_in_Danish_wild.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/tbed.14040","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Animal Welfare and Disease Control"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"university of sydney","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Sydney","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Jung","key":"kjaerpau","last":"Kjaer","locid":"b93ddd14-b340-4550-b3cf-eb9473b0d716","name":"Kjær, Lene Jung","name_org":"Kjaer, Lene Jung","orcid":"0000-0001-9659-4672","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Charlotte Kristiane","key":"hjulsagerpau","last":"Hjulsager","name":"Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars Erik","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"135a9407-d29c-4508-8f79-978f5b295a15","name":"Larsen, Lars Erik","orcid":"0000-0003-0730-5555","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anette Ella","key":"boklundpau","last":"Boklund","locid":"b56cee2e-0320-4c3d-a360-6dc1f5b2ee67","name":"Boklund, Anette","name_org":"Boklund, Anette Ella","orcid":"0000-0001-6359-3403","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tariq","key":"halasapau","last":"Halasa","locid":"8eda71d3-00d4-4316-a46f-20be6613d7a7","name":"Halasa, Tariq","orcid":"0000-0003-1112-0254","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Michael P","key":"wardpau","last":"Ward","name":"Ward, Michael P","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten Thure","key":"kirkebypau","last":"Kirkeby","locid":"ea24c9a4-7aa6-40f3-9afb-0af19f1e962d","name":"Kirkeby, Carsten","name_org":"Kirkeby, Carsten Thure","orcid":"0000-0001-9292-5526","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33600073"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/tbed.14040","issn":["18651674","18651682"],"issn_org":["18651674"],"issue":"2","journal":"Transboundary and Emerging Diseases","pages":"706-719","status":"p","title":"Transboundary and Emerging Diseases","volume":"69"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85127140646"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-57a79472-73dc-45b0-9f09-40d23ff4bb4e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Landscape effects and spatial patterns of avian influenza virus in Danish wild birds, 2006-2020","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-445857","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Although exercise and daily physical activity (PA) have long been known to benefit patients with chronic disorders, knowledge is limited regarding asthma. OBJECTIVE: In a Danish setting, our aim was to measure physical activity, sedentary behavior, and physical capacity among patients with asthma. We hypothesized that people with severe asthma would be less active and more sedentary than their mild-moderate counterparts. METHODS: Adults with asthma were recruited through respiratory outpatient clinics and subsequently examined twice, 4 weeks apart. At each visit, participants underwent a series of lung function tests, questionnaires, and maximum oxygen uptake testing (VO2max). Between the visits, participants wore an accelerometer continuously for 4 weeks, measuring sedentary time and daily steps. Sixty patients, 27 with mild-moderate asthma (GINA 1–3) and 33 with severe asthma (GINA 4–5), completed both visits and had valid accelerometer measurements. RESULTS: No significant differences between the two groups were found in sedentary time, number of steps or VO2max.   VO2max was significantly correlated with FeNO (r = −0.30, p < 0.05), Short Form-12 Mental Health (r = 0.37, p < 0.05), Asthma Control Questionnaire (r = −0.35, p < 0.05), and Mini Asthma Quality of Life Questionnaire (r = 0.36, p < 0.05). CONCLUSION: No differences were observed between patients with mild-moderate and severe asthma regarding sedentary behavior, daily steps or level of cardiopulmonary fitness. Furthermore, patients with the highest VO2max had the higher quality of life scores. Abbreviations: VO2max: Maximal Oxygen Uptake; CPET: Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing; BMI: Body Mass Index; FEV1: Forced Expired Volume in the First Second; FVC: Forced Vital Capacity; PEF: Peak Expiratory Flow; EIB: Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction; COPD: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease; ACQ: Asthma Control Questionnaire; Mini-AQLQ: Mini Asthma Quality of Life Questionnaire; SF-12: Short Form 12 Health Survey; SNOT-22: Sino-Nasal Outcome Test 22; GINA: The Global Initiative for Asthma; CRP: C-reactive Protein; Hgb:Hemoglobin count; EOS: Eosinophil count; EVH: Eucapnic Voluntary Hyperventilation; FeNO: Fractional Exhaled Nitric Oxide; PA: Physical Activity ERS: European Respiratory Society; ATS: American Thoracic Society; CRS: Chronic Rhinosinusitis; AHR: Airway Hyperresponsiveness.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hansen, Nikolaj Brix","Henriksen, Marius","Dall, Christian Have","Vest, Susanne","Larsen, Lotte","Ulrik, Charlotte Suppli","Backer, Vibeke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-516885"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Asthma","Daily steps","Exercise training","Physical activity","Physical capacity","Sedentary Behavior","VOmax"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"physical activity physical capacity and sedentary behavior among asthma patients","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2022.2101599","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-516885","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9467604","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36105719","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85137991645","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-cea46fe4-5bd6-4bfd-aea1-f14e0eb4515f"],"keyword":["Asthma","VOmax","daily steps","exercise training","physical activity","physical capacity","sedentary behavior"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3EF8CAB6655B189FFA5318F72EA7CBC4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2022.2101599","version":"Published Version"},{"a