e2617ff3a","sdu-e8adac9e-9ab6-4b21-95ab-cf8f7bdf616b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Immunogenicity and safety of double dosage of pneumococcal vaccines in adult kidney transplant recipients and waiting list patients: A non-blinded, randomized clinical trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.60-666889","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Use of digital health services, such as digital patient-reported outcomes, depends on many different human factors as well as digital design solutions. One factor is clinicians’ attitude towards the system, their reasoning behind the using system and their perceptions of patients’ ability to engage with digital health systems. This study aimed to explore hospital clinicians’ attitudes towards digital patient-reported outcomes used in the routine care and treatment of inflammatory bowel disease, and to explore the potential role of clinicians’ attitudes in influencing patients’ use of digital patient-reported outcomes. Results: Twelve clinicians using digital patient-reported outcome assessments in the care of inflammatory bowel disease were interviewed about their experiences of, and perspectives on, using this service. Most participants supported the use of digital patient-reported outcome assessments in the care of most patients. Participants reported that most patients found the digital solution easy to use. They perceived digital patient-reported outcomes to have three main purposes: prioritising resources; improving patients’ quality of life; and improving quality of care. The patient-clinician relationship was of great importance to participants. Participants varied in their intention to use digital PRO, as some viewed the system as a positive but optional add-on for patients, whilst others intended to use the system with all eligible patients. Conclusion: Clinicians’ general support of using digital patient-reported outcomes might facilitate their use among patients with inflammatory bowel disease. The participants saw benefits in doing so for patients, clinicians and the wider health service. Clinicians’ attitudes towards the use of digital PRO in the care of their patients may influence patients’ uptake of health service.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard","Appel, Charlotte Weiling","Larsen, Birgit Furstrand","Hanna, Lisa","Kayser, Lars"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-020310"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Deakin University"],"keyword":["Digital health","Digital patient reported outcomes","eHealth"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["University Research Clinic for Innovative Patient Pathways"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"digital patient reported outcomes in inflammatory bowel disease routine clinical practice the clinician perspective","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41687-022-00462-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-020310","identifier":[{"id":"35587297","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85130274231","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9117590","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-68f3e930-223e-4d49-8dcf-98ed9af769e8","ku-d6158288-6cc5-4cd6-a41b-956ce0e32716"],"keyword":["Digital health","Digital patient reported outcomes","eHealth"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BD162D3CB52626747BB85396B482944F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/307730841/Digital.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41687-022-00462-x","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Health Services Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"deakin university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Deakin University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Diagnostisk Center, Universitetsklinik for Innovative Patientforløb, Silkeborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["University Research Clinic for Innovative Patient Pathways"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Amalie Søgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1e0e8e88-821d-4c48-a563-7afe883f8e9b","name":"Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-2884-3902","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte W.","key":"appelpau","last":"Appel","locid":"0d1db098-5494-4dd5-ad41-6c2d6a36a9e4","name":"Appel, Charlotte Weiling","name_org":"Appel, Charlotte W.","orcid":"0000-0003-0397-9739","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Birgit Furstrand","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"2d816040-cec1-44f7-ac67-adf16438df62","name":"Larsen, Birgit Furstrand","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisa","key":"hannapau","last":"Hanna","name":"Hanna, Lisa","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars","key":"kayserpau","last":"Kayser","locid":"94c6679f-d8da-419f-9bd0-186a97295257","name":"Kayser, Lars","orcid":"0000-0002-0909-4088","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35587297"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s41687-022-00462-x","issn":["25098020"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Patient-Reported Outcomes","paperid":"52","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Patient-Reported Outcomes","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85130274231"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-68f3e930-223e-4d49-8dcf-98ed9af769e8","ku-d6158288-6cc5-4cd6-a41b-956ce0e32716"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Digital patient-reported outcomes in inflammatory bowel disease routine clinical practice : the clinician perspective","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-020310","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Use of digital patient-reported outcomes is being introduced in care of chronic conditions, including Inflammatory Bowel Disease. The aim is to supplement face-to-face follow-up sessions through symptom screening, and to inform follow-up through questions about mental health and quality of life. However, little is known about who is using this as intended. This study aimed to map differences between users and non-users among people with IBD and explore the mechanisms behind. We administered a questionnaire including the Readiness and Enablement Index for Health Technology (ReadHy) and the Service User Technology Acceptability Questionnaire (SUTAQ) to all people with IBD registered at Silkeborg Regional Hospital. Comparison between users and non-users and cluster analysis was conducted. Effect size (Cohen’s d) was used to estimate magnitude of difference between groups. The user and non-user groups differed most strongly by level of emotional distress (d = 0.45). Cluster analysis of the ReadHy scales showed profiles have different sets of difficulties and reservations towards digital solutions. These difficulties correlated moderately with SUTAQ dissatisfaction and low acceptability. The dimensions of ReadHy may help to better understand particular needs of people with IBD when accessing digital PROs, which may lead to higher acceptability and improved quality of care.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard","Hanna, Lisa","Larsen, Birgit Furstrand","Appel, Charlotte Weiling","Osborne, Richard H.","Kayser, Lars"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-022634"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Deakin University","Swinburne University of Technology","University of Cambridge"],"keyword":["Ambulatory Care Facilities","Chronic Disease","Human factors","Humans","Inflammatory Bowel Diseases therapy","Patient Reported Outcome Measures","Quality of Life","Telecare","Telehealth","Telemedicine methods","eHealth","healthcare service innovation and IT"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"readiness acceptance and use of digital patient reported outcome in an outpatient clinic","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/14604582221106000","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-022634","identifier":[{"id":"35658693","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85131313512","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-88dc3c33-1bb3-4aea-9e81-7cc21780f695","ku-df70debe-5731-400d-90be-adef95b624a5"],"keyword":["Ambulatory Care Facilities","Chronic Disease","Humans","Inflammatory Bowel Diseases/therapy","Patient Reported Outcome Measures","Quality of Life","Telemedicine/methods","ehealth","healthcare service innovation and IT","human factors","telecare","telehealth"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-42D304F460969C42A21688C283F0BE99"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/14604582221106000","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/310772644/Read.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Health Services Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"deakin university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Deakin University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Diagnostisk Center, Universitetsklinik for Innovative Patientforløb, Silkeborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"au","key":"swinburne university of technology","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Swinburne University of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Amalie Søgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1e0e8e88-821d-4c48-a563-7afe883f8e9b","name":"Nielsen, Amalie Søgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-2884-3902","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisa","key":"hannapau","last":"Hanna","name":"Hanna, Lisa","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Birgit Furstrand","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"2d816040-cec1-44f7-ac67-adf16438df62","name":"Larsen, Birgit Furstrand","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte W.","key":"appelpau","last":"Appel","locid":"0d1db098-5494-4dd5-ad41-6c2d6a36a9e4","name":"Appel, Charlotte Weiling","name_org":"Appel, Charlotte W.","orcid":"0000-0003-0397-9739","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","first":"Richard H","key":"osbornepau","last":"Osborne","locid":"591fb1b7-c517-49c3-b6db-251efd629bdc","name":"Osborne, Richard H.","name_org":"Osborne, Richard H","orcid":"0000-0002-9081-2699","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars","key":"kayserpau","last":"Kayser","locid":"94c6679f-d8da-419f-9bd0-186a97295257","name":"Kayser, Lars","orcid":"0000-0002-0909-4088","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35658693"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/14604582221106000","issn":["14604582","17412811"],"issn_org":["14604582"],"issue":"2","journal":"Health Informatics Journal","publisher":"SAGE Publications Ltd","status":"p","title":"Health Informatics Journal","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131313512"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-88dc3c33-1bb3-4aea-9e81-7cc21780f695","ku-df70debe-5731-400d-90be-adef95b624a5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Readiness, acceptance and use of digital patient reported outcome in an outpatient clinic","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-022634","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Porcine circovirus type 2 (PCV2) and Lawsonia intracellularis infections can cause enteritis in pigs. A Danish study showed a significantly higher probability of detecting PCV2 without concurrent L. intracellularis infection, indicating that one of these pathogens has an impact on the dynamics of the other. Therefore, a delayed co-infection model was set up, initially aiming at investigating the interaction between PCV2 and L. intracellularis in pigs challenged with PCV2 and 2 weeks later with L. intracellularis. But due to PCV2 contamination of the L. intracellularis inoculum the aim was revisited to describing the infection dynamics and pathogenesis of pigs infected with PCV2 followed by delayed simultaneous exposure to PCV2 and L. intracellularis. Twenty-four high-health piglets were divided into three groups of eight pigs (A, B, C) and inoculated at experimental day (EXD) 0 with mock (groups A and B) or PCV2 (group C), and at EXD 14 with mock (group A) or L. intracellularis/PCV2 (groups B and C). The pigs underwent daily clinical examination, and were necropsied at EXD 51–52. Furthermore, histology, immunohistochemistry, serology and PCR for PCV2 and L. intracellularis, and measurement of C-reactive protein were carried out. Results: Group A remained negative for PCV2 and L. intracellularis. Following inoculation with L. intracellularis/PCV2, no significant differences were observed between group B and C, however pigs already infected with PCV2 (group C) showed milder clinical signs and exhibited milder intestinal lesions, less shedding of L. intracellularis and developed higher L. intracellularis antibody titers than the pigs in group B that only received the combined infection. Though the differences between group B and C were non-significant, all results pointed in the same direction, indicating that the pigs in group B were more affected by the L. intracellularis infection compared to the pigs in group C. Conclusions: Previous exposure to PCV2 had limited impact on the subsequent exposure to a combined L. intracellularis/PCV2 inoculation. However, there was a tendency that the infection dynamics of PCV2 and development of antibodies to PCV2 and L. intracellularis were altered in pigs previously exposed to PCV2. These differences should be confirmed in further experimental trials.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Hansen, Mette Sif","Jensen, Tim Kåre","Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","Angen, Øystein","Riber, Ulla","Nielsen, Jens","Heegaard, Peter M. H.","Larsen, Lars Erik"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-034092"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Hansen, Mette Sif"],"keyword":["Delayed co infection model","Experimental infection","Lawsonia intracellularis","Porcine circovirus type 2 PCV2","Porcine enteritis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"experimental infection of high health pigs with porcine circovirus type 000000000002 pcv000000000002 and i lawsonia intracellularis i","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fvets.2022.994147","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-034092","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9583870","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85140486724","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36277064","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000873756100001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["dtu-424cb901-8e37-4f8e-866e-42eb045427f6","ku-a4b14801-4fde-4501-ab0b-55e2fc925df3"],"keyword":["Delayed co-infection model","Experimental infection","Lawsonia intracellularis","Porcine circovirus type 2(PCV2)","Porcine enteritis"],"lang":"English","level":"Sci