"Københavns Universitet","status":"p","title":"CLASS - Folkeskolelæreres arbejdsmiljø under anden bølge af COVID-19-pandemien i Danmark,"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4c6324e2-a3e2-4fc7-b392-111e4c3dc9db"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"CLASS - Folkeskolelæreres arbejdsmiljø under anden bølge af COVID-19-pandemien i Danmark","type":"Report Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-438986","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Antibiotic (AB) consumption in production animals has a high awareness among politicians and consumers due to the risk of selection for AB resistance among potentially zoonotic bacteria. However, AB treatment of animals is at times necessary to treat diseases and ensure the wellbeing of the animals we take into our care. Raised without antibiotics (RWA) is a concept where pigs are individually ear-tagged for tracking, and if pigs are AB treated, they lose their RWA status. At slaughter, the farmer receives an additional price for non-AB treated pigs. The objective of this study was to identify risk factors for AB treatment and to investigate growth performance of pigs in two Danish RWA herds. Results: A total of 518 pigs in herd A and 436 pigs in herd B, were individually ear-tagged and subjected to weekly investigations of AB treatment status from birth to 12 weeks of age. Bodyweight was recorded at birth, 2, 4 and 12 weeks of age. The results showed, that at 12 weeks of age, 82 of 518 liveborn pigs were AB treated in herd A and 31 of 436 liveborn pigs were AB treated in herd B. Individual pigs that required AB treatment had a reduced average daily gain from day 0 to 28 in both herds (herd A, P < 0.001; herd B, P = 0.062) and from day 0 to 84 in herd A (P < 0.001). Additionally, significant risk factors for AB treatment were identified as a low bodyweight in herd A, whereas barrows and litters with less than 19 piglets were the main risk factors in herd B. Conclusion: The results suggests that in order to reduce AB treatments particular attention should be addressed to smaller pigs and barrows in RWA herds. In these two Danish RWA herds from this study it was possible for 64 and 68% pigs to reach 12 weeks of life without any AB treatments.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Lynegaard, Julie C.","Larsen, Inge","Hansen, C. F.","Nielsen, Jens Peter","Williams, Charlotte Amdi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-477504"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Antibiotics","Bodyweight","Diarrhoea","GROWTH","Gender","Pigs"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"performance and risk factors associated with first antibiotic treatment in two herds raising pigs without antibiotics","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40813-021-00198-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-477504","identifier":[{"id":"33597035","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85101164971","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC7888151","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-8c94bded-576f-46c2-b4a1-de0bd291e10a"],"keyword":["Antibiotics","Bodyweight","Diarrhoea","Gender","Growth","Pigs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31951D1EF959C192D6A17845C25E7004"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40813-021-00198-y","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/257873417/s40813_021_00198_y.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Production, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"J. C.","key":"lynegaardpau","last":"Lynegaard","locid":"bcf39ade-982e-4e66-a1c1-9a943aeb16b0","name":"Lynegaard, Julie C.","name_org":"Lynegaard, J. C.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"I.","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"82b09bf9-73d5-4c71-a3c1-01e66b0e9ebe","name":"Larsen, Inge","name_org":"Larsen, I.","orcid":"0000-0003-4575-2241","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"C. F.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, C. F.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"J. P.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"e550301c-f89e-4a8b-bf34-e6fc3b76ff37","name":"Nielsen, Jens Peter","name_org":"Nielsen, J. P.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"C.","key":"amdipau","last":"Amdi","locid":"8057561d-85f5-4717-ba1e-18a709a9f121","name":"Williams, Charlotte Amdi","name_org":"Amdi, C.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33597035"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s40813-021-00198-y","issn":["20555660"],"issue":"1","journal":"Porcine Health Management","paperid":"18","status":"p","title":"Porcine Health Management","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101164971"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8c94bded-576f-46c2-b4a1-de0bd291e10a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Performance and risk factors associated with first antibiotic treatment in two herds, raising pigs without antibiotics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-477504","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Multiple myeloma (MM) is a heterogeneous plasma cell malignancy differing substantially in clinical behavior, prognosis, and response to treatment. With the advent of novel therapies, many patients achieve long-lasting remissions, but some experience aggressive and treatment refractory relapses. So far, MM is considered incurable. Myeloma pathogenesis can broadly be explained by two interacting mechanisms, intraclonal evolution of cancer cells and development of an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment. Failures in isotype class switching and somatic hypermutations result in the neoplastic transformation typical of MM and other B cell malignancies. Interestingly, although genetic alterations occur and evolve over time, they are also present in premalignant stages, which never progress to MM, suggesting that genetic mutations are necessary but not sufficient for myeloma transformation. Changes in composition and function of the immune cells are associated with loss of effective immune surveillance, which might represent another mechanism driving malignant transformation. During the last decade, the traditional view on myeloma treatment has changed dramatically. It is increasingly evident that treatment strategies solely based on targeting intrinsic properties of myeloma cells are insufficient. Lately, approaches that redirect the cells of the otherwise suppressed immune system to take control over myeloma have emerged. Evidence of utility of this principle was initially established by the observation of the graft-versus-myeloma effect in allogeneic stem cell-transplanted patients. A variety of new strategies to harness both innate and antigen-specific immunity against MM have recently been developed and intensively tested in clinical trials. This review aims to give readers a basic understanding of how the immune system can be engaged to treat MM, to summarize the main immunotherapeutic modalities, their current role in clinical care, and future prospects.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Krejcik, Jakub","Barnkob, Mike Bogetofte","Nyvold, Charlotte Guldborg","Larsen, Thomas Stauffer","Barington, Torben","Abildgaard, Niels"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-589862"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Abildgaard, Niels"],"keyword":["Adoptive cell transfer","Allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Immune modulation","Immunogenic cell death","Immunomodulatory drugs","Immunotherapy","Monoclonal antibodies","Multiple myeloma"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"harnessing the immune system to fight multiple myeloma","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers13184546","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-589862","identifier":[{"id":"34572773","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85115028872","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8467095","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-3a302f00-4ace-4b3a-859c-8f409ce8210f"],"keyword":["Adoptive cell transfer","Allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Cancer vaccination","Immune modulation","Immunogenic cell death","Immunomodulatory drugs","Immunotherapy","Monoclonal antibodies","Multiple myeloma"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C1546AC731699C3BC6C025CFFA2BEE6E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/191973042/cancers_13_04546_v2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers13184546","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, CITCO","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Haematology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakub","key":"krejcikpau","last":"Krejcik","locid":"7335d44c-5031-4ead-ba45-02ad067ab673","name":"Krejcik, Jakub","orcid":"0000-0003-1833-583X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mike Bogetofte","key":"barnkobpau","last":"Barnkob","locid":"94b55950-aece-458e-bac0-7d54fcdf4435","name":"Barnkob, Mike Bogetofte","orcid":"0000-0002-5892-1174","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Guldborg","key":"nyvoldpau","last":"Nyvold","locid":"ba584c2b-215e-421c-a6e6-c799c1b16b7c","name":"Nyvold, Charlotte Guldborg","orcid":"0000-0002-0411-7594","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Thomas Stauffer","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"dd2de49b-c3d9-49cb-bb8d-e92119e0b2d1","name":"Larsen, Thomas Stauffer","orcid":"0000-0002-8418-6708","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torben","key":"baringtonpau","last":"Barington","locid":"1038a536-fae8-4794-a15e-bffe30eb51f7","name":"Barington, Torben","orcid":"0000-0001-6968-148X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"abildgaardpau","last":"Abildgaard","locid":"595418b9-29f8-4fd3-8663-1b32eab3451a","name":"Abildgaard, Niels","orcid":"0000-0001-6852-2014","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34572773"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/cancers13184546","issn":["20726694"],"issue":"18","journal":"Cancers","paperid":"4546","publisher":"MDPI","status":"p","title":"Cancers","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115028872"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3a302f00-4ace-4b3a-859c-8f409ce8210f"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Harnessing the Immune System to Fight Multiple Myeloma","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-589862","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: Children undergoing surgery and general anesthesia often experience preoperative anxiety (POA) with related negative short-, medium- and long-term consequences. Anxiolytic premedication has negative side effects, and nonpharmacologic interventions are often resource demanding and not always readily available in a busy clinical setting. The use of an age-appropriate game on a tablet computer may reduce POA, postoperative pain, and occurrence of emergence delirium (ED). Design: Children aged 3 to 6 years scheduled to undergo elective minor surgery were randomly assigned to play a game on a tablet computer while in the holding area before anesthesia (n = 30) or prepared as per departmental standard only (n = 30). Methods: POA, ED, and levels of pain were assessed by the modified Yale Preoperative Anxiety Scale, Pediatric Anesthesia Emergence Delirium, and Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability scale, respectively. Findings: A total of 60 children were randomized to either the intervention group or the control group. Gender, bodyweight, duration of anesthesia and surgery, and fentanyl dosages were comparable between the two groups. Tablet-gaming children tended to be less anxious than control subjects at the time of anesthesia induction (modified Yale Preoperative Anxiety Scale, 55.7 vs 65.8; 95% confidence interval, −0.63 to 20.8; P = .066). There was no difference in occurrence of ED or pain 20 minutes after arrival in the postanesthesia care unit. Conclusions: Although not statistically significant, the use of an age-appropriate tablet computer game may reduce the level of anxiety at the anesthetic induction in 3 to 6 years old children undergoing elective day-case surgery. However, the occurrence of ED and levels of pain appeared unaffected. Standardization of nonpharmacologic interventions to reduce perioperative anxiety and pain is required.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Clausen, Nicola Groes","Madsen, Dorthe","Rosenkilde, Charlotte","Hasfeldt-hansen, Dorthe","Larsen, Line Gry","Hansen, Tom Giedsing"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-600673"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Clausen, Nicola Groes"],"keyword":["Emergence delirium","General anesthesia","Pediatric anesthesia","preoperative anxiety"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the use of tablet computers to reduce preoperative anxiety in children before anesthesia a randomized controlled study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85101547506","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-600673","identifier":[{"id":"33637409","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85101547506","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-5ff9b411-8c04-4dd7-b46f-e64434180560"],"keyword":["emergence delirium","general anesthesia","pediatric anesthesia","preoperative anxiety"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F0CA9667BADBFC575963771FA74FE12D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicola Groes","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","locid":"66d531b1-92e2-4511-ac5f-1857e34cc1ce","name":"Clausen, Nicola Groes","orcid":"0000-0002-3590-5171","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dorthe","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","name":"Madsen, Dorthe","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte","key":"rosenkildepau","last":"Rosenkilde","locid":"9ad4c026-4569-46dc-ad06-af7882dff170","name":"Rosenkilde, Charlotte","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dorthe","key":"hasfeldthansenpau","last":"Hasfeldt-Hansen","locid":"69b73aac-113b-4875-927c-d56644279012","name":"Hasfeldt-hansen, Dorthe","name_org":"Hasfeldt-Hansen, Dorthe","orcid":"0009-0006-5960-7554","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Gry","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"9e779ea0-e653-4cd5-94e0-a583ca1eb0e6","name":"Larsen, Line Gry","orcid":"0000-0002-0683-4503","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tom Giedsing","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"5f66539d-cd24-4c21-8e6b-fefbcdcb0032","name":"Hansen, Tom Giedsing","orcid":"0000-0002-5896-9768","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33637409"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jopan.2020.09.012","issn":["10899472","15328473"],"issn_org":["10899472"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of PeriAnesthesia Nursing","pages":"275-278","publisher":"WB  Saunders","publisher_org":"W.B. Saunders","status":"p","title":"Journal of PeriAnesthesia Nursing","volume":"36"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101547506"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-5ff9b411-8c04-4dd7-b46f-e64434180560"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The Use of Tablet Computers to Reduce Preoperative Anxiety in Children Before Anesthesia : A Randomized Controlled Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-600673","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Computational protein design has taken big strides over the recent years, however, the tools available are still not at a state where a sequence can be designed to fold into a given protein structure at will and with high probability. We have here applied a recent release of Rosetta Design to redesign a set of structurally very similar proteins belonging to the Thioredoxin fold. We determined design success using a combination of a genetic screening tool to assay folding/stability in E. coli and selecting the best hits from this for further biochemical characterization. We have previously used this set of template proteins for redesign and found that success was highly dependent on template structure, a trait which was also found in this study. Nevertheless, state of the art design software is now able to predict the best template, most likely due to the introduction of the cart_bonded energy term. The template that led to the greatest fraction of successful designs was the same (a Thioredoxin from spinach) as that identified in our previous study. Our previously described redesign of Thioredoxin, which also used the spinach protein as template, however also performed well. In the present study, both these templates yielded proteins with compact folded structures, and enforces the conclusion that any design project must carefully consider different design templates. Fortunately, selecting designs using the cart_bonded energy term appears to correctly identify such templates.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Marin, Frederikke Isa","Johansson, K. E.","O'Shea, Charlotte","Lindorff-Larsen, Kresten","Winther, Jakob R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677792"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"computational and experimental assessment of backbone templates for computational protein design","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.06.23.449573","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677792","ids":["ku-2901af10-6086-4f25-93ba-5381f96c1cbc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-8C5CBE40D4D27EE83E26EDE21F7EDCCA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/359238580/2021.06.23.449573v1.full.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.06.23.449573","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Biology, Biomolecular Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"F. I.","key":"marinpau","last":"Marin","locid":"bd816760-1e9c-4534-98f2-4214152fbc7c","name":"Marin, Frederikke Isa","name_org":"Marin, F. I.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"K. E.","key":"johanssonpau","last":"Johansson","locid":"a4bce7a7-287d-4a96-815a-a2fe34a835f9","name":"Johansson, K. E.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"C.","key":"osheapau","last":"O’Shea","locid":"66faa368-fff8-4a0a-8001-73ce7306646f","name":"O'Shea, Charlotte","name_org":"O’Shea, C.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"K.","key":"lindorfflarsenpau","last":"Lindorff-Larsen","locid":"be20a4d8-92c4-44b9-9dfb-b6ad6471a58c","name":"Lindorff-Larsen, Kresten","name_org":"Lindorff-Larsen, K.","orcid":"0000-0002-4750-6039","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"J.R.","key":"wintherpau","last":"Winther","locid":"1037684b-136c-4b11-b63a-20b7f13a853b","name":"Winther, Jakob R.","name_org":"Winther, J.R.","orcid":"0000-0001-6995-9154","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1101/2021.06.23.449573","pages":"15","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-2901af10-6086-4f25-93ba-5381f96c1cbc"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Computational and experimental assessment of backbone templates for computational protein design","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677792","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Interactions between host and gut microbial communities are modulated by diets and play pivotal roles in immunological homeostasis and health. We show that exchanging the protein source in a high fat, high sugar, westernized diet from casein to whole-cell lysates of the non-commensal bacterium Methylococcus capsulatus Bath is sufficient to reverse western diet-induced changes in the gut microbiota to a state resembling that of lean, low fat diet-fed mice, both under mild thermal stress (T22 °C) and at thermoneutrality (T30 °C). Concomitant with microbiota changes, mice fed the Methylococcus-based western diet exhibit improved glucose regulation, reduced body and liver fat, and diminished hepatic immune infiltration. Intake of the Methylococcu-based diet markedly boosts Parabacteroides abundances in a manner depending on adaptive immunity, and upregulates triple positive (Foxp3+RORγt+IL-17+) regulatory T cells in the small and large intestine. Collectively, these data point to the potential for leveraging the use of McB lysates to improve immunometabolic homeostasis.","author_count":25,"contributors":["Jensen, Benjamin A.H.","Holm, Jacob B.","Larsen, Ida S.","von Burg, Nicole","Derer, Stefanie","Sonne, Si B.","Pærregaard, Simone Isling","Damgaard, Mads V.","Indrelid, Stine A.","Rivollier, Aymeric Marie Christian","Agrinier, Anne Laure","Sulek, Karolina","Arnoldussen, Yke J.","Fjære, Even","Marette, André","Angell, Inga L.","Rudi, Knut","Treebak, Jonas T.","Madsen, Lise","Åkesson, Caroline Piercey","Agace, William Winston","Sina, Christian","Kleiveland, Charlotte R.","Kristiansen, Karsten","Lea, Tor E."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-035643"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jensen, Benjamin A.H."],"country":["Canada","China","Germany","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":