{"abstract":"In a commercial diagnostic setting streamlining and optimization is an important factor when the goal is to provide high quality diagnostic results while remaining competitive. In the PCR diagnostics unit at DTU National Veterinary Institute part of this optimization programme is to replace conventional PCR assays with real-time PCR assays to obtain a uniform assay palette. The present work describes the development of a novel real-time RT-qPCR assay for the dual detection of canine and phocine distemper virus. The assay is relevant for the future detection of outbreaks of canine distemper virus in e.g. in farmed mink and wildlife and phocine distemper in seals. A set of primers and dual labelled probe was designed based on an alignment of distemper sequences in GenBank from various species and in-house sequences from recent outbreaks in Danish farmed mink. The assay amplifies a segment of 151 bp in the Phosphoprotein (P) gene of the distemper virus genome. The dynamic range and PCR efficiency (E) was experimentally determined using 10-fold dilutions of a specially designed distemper DNA-oligo in addition to extracted RNA from clinical samples. E of the real-time assay was shown to range between 0.95 and 1.05 when using both clinical samples and the distemper DNA-oligo. Comparing the real-time RT-PCR assay to a currently used conventional assay showed that the real-time assay detects a 102 –fold further dilution of a clinical sample. Tests of distemper positive clinical samples from a wide range of different species performed in parallel with the conventional PCR assay revealed that whereas both assays detect canine and phocine distemper virus, only the conventional assay could detect the dolphin distemper variant. Furthermore it was shown that the Taqman assay does not amplify influenza, a common differential diagnosis in mink and seals. Further testing is needed to determine the effects of PCR inhibitors in clinical samples. In addition, an elaborate panel of potential differential diagnostic agents must be tested in order to determine the assay specificity.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nielsen, Linette Buxbom","Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","Larsen, Helene"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-378648"],"event":{"end":"2016-10-21","place":"London, gb","start":"2016-10-20","title":"4<sup>th </sup>qPCR &amp; Digital PCR Congress","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"development of a novel real time qpcr assay for the dual detection of canine and phocine distemper virus","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/127426579/Poster_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-378648","ids":["dtu-f79de981-fc29-4e26-9597-ca7e100a4d21"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-45A748D254EFC21AF6E0D63BA30A2079"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/127426579/Poster_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Veterinary Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Linette Buxbom","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"0c692353-e3da-4c8c-ad04-5489c4491d4a","name":"Nielsen, Linette Buxbom","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Kristiane","key":"hjulsagerpau","last":"Hjulsager","locid":"6c1511ef-8211-4653-8c81-cd55b7f63f64","name":"Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","orcid":"0000-0001-7557-8876","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Helene","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"42a4eb79-1cad-4fcc-876c-8d01f35c8389","name":"Larsen, Helene","orcid":"0000-0001-5065-4967","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-f79de981-fc29-4e26-9597-ca7e100a4d21"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Development of a novel real-time qPCR assay for the dual detection of canine and phocine distemper virus","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-378648","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Background Some herpesviruses like human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) encode viral G protein-coupled receptors that cause reprogramming of cell signaling to facilitate dissemination of the virus, prevent immune surveillance and establish life-long latency. Human GPCRs are known to function in complex signaling networks involving direct physical interactions as well as indirect crosstalk of orthogonal signaling networks. The human chemokine receptor CXCR4 is expressed on hematopoietic stem cells, leukocytes, endothelial and epithelial cells, which are infected by HCMV or display reservoirs of latency. Results We investigated the potential heteromerization of US28 with CXCR4 as well as the influence of US28 on CXCR4 signaling. Using Bioluminescence Resonance Energy Transfer and luciferase-complementation based methods we show that US28 expression exhibits negative effects on CXCR4 signaling and constitutive surface expression in HEK293T cells. Furthermore, we demonstrate that this effect is not mediated by receptor heteromerization but via signaling crosstalk. Additionally, we show that in HCMV, strain TB40E, infected HUVEC the surface expression of CXCR4 is strongly downregulated, whereas in TB40E-delUS28 infected cells, CXCR4 surface expression is not altered in particular at late time points of infection. Conclusions We show that the vGPCR US28 is leading to severely disturbed signaling and surface expression of the chemokine receptor CXCR4 thereby representing an effective mechanism used by vGPCRs to reprogram host cell signaling. In contrast to other studies, we demonstrate that these effects are not mediated via heteromerization.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Frank, Theresa","Reichel, Anna","Larsen, Olav","Stilp, Anne-Charlotte","Rosenkilde, Mette M.","Stamminger, Thomas","Ozawa, Takeaki","Tschammer, Nuska"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-497834"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Chemokine receptor CXCR4","Constitutive activity","Signaling crosstalk"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"attenuation of chemokine receptor function and surface expression as an immunomodulatory strategy employed by human cytomegalovirus is linked to vgpcr us000000000028","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12964-016-0154-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-497834","identifier":[{"id":"000390281400001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85004147083","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27955674","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC5153698","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-aecc52f4-d3eb-4796-b11b-df55002acc84"],"keyword":["Bioluminescence complementation","Bioluminescence resonance energy transfer","Chemokine receptor CXCR4","Constitutive activity","Signaling crosstalk","Viral G protein-coupled receptor US28"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BCCBD737380AF3BE175551A930C44B1D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12964-016-0154-x","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/171655975/art_3A10.1186_2Fs12964_016_0154_x.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Neuroscience, Molpharm Lab"],"nora_code