-100.00-034778","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Purpose – It is important within public health goals to promote adolescents’ mental health and to reduce socioeconomic inequalities in mental health. Among adults there are indications that the socioeconomic pattern of low positive mental health (PMH) differs from the socioeconomic pattern of high PMH. Knowledge regarding the social epidemiology of PMH among young people is lacking. The purpose of this paper is to examine the socioeconomic patterning of aspects of low and high PMH among adolescents. Design/methodology/approach – The Health Behaviour in School-aged Children Methodology Development Study 2012 provided data on 3,670 adolescents aged 11-15 in two municipalities in Denmark. Socioeconomic differences in aspects of low and high PMH (self-esteem, social competence and self-efficacy) were investigated by calculating sex-specific prevalence of PMH in socioeconomic groups measured by parents’ occupational social class. Using multi-level logistic regression analyses, odds ratios for low and high PMH compared to moderate PMH were estimated. Findings – In age-adjusted analyses there seemed to be a graded relationship with increasing odds for low PMH with decreasing socioeconomic position, but no indication of a socioeconomic patterning of high PMH. The prevalence of high self-esteem and high self-efficacy was higher among boys than girls. High social competence and high self-efficacy increased with age. Research limitations/implications – Public health research has primarily focused on risk factors and mental health problems. Research highlighting more detailed aspects of PMH is needed. Originality/value – The socioeconomic pattern of high PMH may be different from the socioeconomic pattern of low PMH.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nielsen, Line","Stewart-Brown, Sarah","Vinther-Larsen, Mathilde","Meilstrup, Charlotte","Holstein, Bjørn Evald","Koushede, Vibeke"],"cuid":["S03-092.50-735476"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Warwick"],"keyword":["Mental wellbeing","Positive mental health","Self efficacy","Self esteem","Social competence","Socioeconomic inequalities"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high and low levels of positive mental health are there socioeconomic differences among adolescents","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84961875666","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-092.50-735476","identifier":[{"id":"84961875666","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-37288153-0e9a-407f-b844-2ef7271fda98","ku-8d455ce2-4e73-46ec-b824-84cd7b0e6f54","sdu-c674079c-009e-47c7-9eee-3adb0c51bd71"],"keyword":["Mental wellbeing","Positive mental health","Self-efficacy","Self-esteem","Social competence","Socioeconomic inequalities"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-1268180433310FCDA17E4373A9B15EB3"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Department of Health and Social Context"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of warwick","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Warwick","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Psychology, Department of Psychology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"70e5e888-ca96-4dae-b64a-36817ddc113b","name":"Nielsen, Line","orcid":"0000-0002-4127-4361","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sarah","key":"stewartbrownpau","last":"Stewart-Brown","name":"Stewart-Brown, Sarah","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mathilde","key":"vintherlarsenpau","last":"Vinther-Larsen","locid":"2ed449a2-142b-47de-9899-efa1dccbd166","name":"Vinther-Larsen, Mathilde","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte Bjerre","key":"meilstruppau","last":"Meilstrup","locid":"71e55e89-4123-4012-b917-9ba55f543556","name":"Meilstrup, Charlotte","name_org":"Meilstrup, Charlotte Bjerre","orcid":"0000-0001-5705-1288","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bjørn Evald","key":"holsteinpau","last":"Holstein","locid":"303aa8e2-44ee-4317-ab4a-8f64355f7bf5","name":"Holstein, Bjørn Evald","orcid":"0000-0002-4348-280X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vibeke","key":"koushedepau","last":"Koushede","locid":"67798c78-14a3-4025-83ec-311b56fc0d15","name":"Koushede, Vibeke","orcid":"0000-0002-7302-1334","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1108/jpmh-10-2014-0041","issn":["17465729","20428731"],"issn_org":["17465729"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Public Mental Health","pages":"37-49","publisher":"Emerald Group Publishing","status":"p","title":"Journal of Public Mental Health","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84961875666"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-37288153-0e9a-407f-b844-2ef7271fda98","ku-8d455ce2-4e73-46ec-b824-84cd7b0e6f54","sdu-c674079c-009e-47c7-9eee-3adb0c51bd71"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"High and low levels of positive mental health : are there socioeconomic differences among adolescents?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-092.50-735476","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Simulation training is a revolutionary addition to health care education. However, developing simulation-based training programs is often dictated by those simulators that are commercially available. Curriculum development requires deliberate planning and a standardized approach, including a 'general needs assessment'. OBJECTIVES: The aim of this study was to perform a national general needs assessment to identify technical procedures in pulmonary medicine that should be integrated in a simulation-based curriculum. METHODS: A three-round Delphi process was initiated among 62 key opinion leaders. Round 1 was an open-ended question to identify technical procedures pulmonologists should learn. Round 2 was a survey using a newly developed needs assessment formula to explore the frequency of procedures, number of operators, risk or discomfort when performed by an inexperienced doctor, and feasibility of simulation-based training. In round 3, results were reviewed and ranked according to priority. RESULTS: The response rates for the three rounds were 74, 63, and 60%, respectively. The Delphi process reduced the 30 procedures identified in round 1 to 11 prioritized technical procedures in round 3. These were: flexible bronchoscopy, pleurocentesis, endobronchial ultrasound, endoscopic ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration, noninvasive ventilation treatment, transthoracic biopsy of pleural or lung tumor, focused ultrasound scanning of the lungs, chest tube insertion, needle biopsy of visible lymph node/tumor of the skin, focused ultrasound scanning of the heart, and thoracoscopy. CONCLUSION: We performed a Delphi study using a needs assessment formula, which identified 11 technical procedures that are highly suitable for simulation-based training. Medical educators can use this list as a resource in planning simulation-based training programs for trainees in pulmonary medicine.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nayahangan, Leizl Joy","Clementsen,