{"abstract":"Gastrointestinal (GI) disorders is the main cause for submitting mink (Neovison vison) carcasses for post-mortem examination at the National Veterinary Institute in Denmark and has been described as the predominant cause of disease and mortality in the Danish mink production (Rattenborg et al. 1999). Diarrhea in mink can be caused by infectious agents (virus, bacteria and parasites) and food-related/multifactorial conditions. Known enteric viral infections are mink enteritis virus (MEV) and mink astrovirus. Coronaviruses and caliciviruses have also been implicated as potential causes or contributors to diarrhea in mink. Rotavirus is poorly described in mink, but has previously been demonstrated in feces from mink pups with and without clinical signs (Jorgensen et al. 1996). The pathogenicity of these viruses could be related to viral load, virulence and the age of the mink. Therefore, there is a need for a quantitative diagnostic approach. We have developed new or adapted previously published real-time PCR/RT-PCR assays for MEV, astrovirus, rota- and coronavirus diagnostics. The technical test validation was initially carried out on archived diarrhea samples from diagnosed positive animals and on normal and diarrhea samples from a case-control study. In order to further validate the applicability of the assays, a testing scheme for normal and affected farms was set up and initiated in June 2015. This protocol will allow optimization of test characteristics (sensitivity, specificity and predictive value) and assessment of the validity of using pooled samples in order to reduce test costs.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hartby, Christina Marie","Kvisgaard, Lise Kirstine","Larsen, Lars Erik","Chriél, Mariann","Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-319985"],"event":{"end":"2015-10-01","place":"Turku, fi","start":"2015-09-29","title":"Nordic Association of Agricultural Scientists","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"application of qpcr assays for diagnosing causes of viral mink diarrhea preliminary results","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/116924292/NJF_qPCR_assays_for_viral_mink_diarrhea_endelig_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-319985","ids":["dtu-33bb9dc5-938c-4a9c-af92-35e7b8fea19a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3AA953CCB4CABB97DCCF0679CD44B33B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/116924292/NJF_qPCR_assays_for_viral_mink_diarrhea_endelig_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Veterinary Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christina Marie","key":"hartbypau","last":"Hartby","locid":"91d05451-70ef-459e-8781-4fa025583e88","name":"Hartby, Christina Marie","orcid":"0000-0003-2852-970X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lise Kirstine","key":"kvisgaardpau","last":"Kvisgaard","locid":"58ce239f-6c30-44e0-ab54-f017a1821425","name":"Kvisgaard, Lise Kirstine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Erik","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"09c2a33d-b353-44de-a850-f3c7dec879cb","name":"Larsen, Lars Erik","orcid":"0000-0003-0730-5555","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mariann","key":"chriélpau","last":"Chriél","locid":"1a5d6261-6bd2-4940-baab-5d5fcc3c0f7c","name":"Chriél, Mariann","orcid":"0000-0001-5306-4495","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Kristiane","key":"hjulsagerpau","last":"Hjulsager","locid":"6c1511ef-8211-4653-8c81-cd55b7f63f64","name":"Hjulsager, Charlotte Kristiane","orcid":"0000-0001-7557-8876","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-33bb9dc5-938c-4a9c-af92-35e7b8fea19a"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Application of qPCR assays for diagnosing causes of viral mink diarrhea. Preliminary results","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-319985","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Background: Sodium selenite at high dose exerts antitumor effects and increases efficacy of cytostatic drugs in multiple preclinical malignancy models. We assessed the safety and efficacy of intravenous administered sodium selenite in cancer patients' refractory to cytostatic drugs in a phase I trial. Patients received first line of chemotherapy following selenite treatment to investigate altered sensitivity to these drugs and preliminary assessment of any clinical benefits. Materials and Methods: Thirty-four patients with different therapy resistant tumors received iv sodium selenite daily for consecutive five days either for two weeks or four weeks. Each cohort consisted of at least three patients who received the same daily dose of selenite throughout the whole treatment. If 0/3 patients had dose-limiting toxicities (DLTs), the study proceeded to the next dose-level. If 2/3 had DLT, the dose was considered too high and if 1/3 had DLT, three more patients were included. Dose-escalation continued until the maximum tolerated dose (MTD) was reached. MTD was defined as the highest dose-level on which 0/3 or 1/6 patients experienced DLT. The primary endpoint was safety, dose-limiting toxic effects and the MTD of sodium selenite. The secondary endpoint was primary response evaluation. Results and Conclusion: MTD was defined as 10.2 mg/m(2), with a calculated median plasma half-life of 18.25 h. The maximum plasma concentration of selenium from a single dose of selenite increased in a nonlinear pattern. The most common adverse events were fatigue, nausea, and cramps in fingers and legs. DLTs were acute, of short duration and reversible. Biomarkers for organ functions indicated no major systemic toxicity. In conclusion, sodium selenite is safe and tolerable when administered up to 10.2 mg/m(2) under current protocol. Further development of the study is underway to determine if prolonged infusions might be a more effective treatment strategy.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Brodin, Ola","Eksborg, Staffan","Wallenberg, Marita","Asker-Hagelberg, Charlotte","Larsen, Erik Huusfeldt","Mohlkert, Dag","Lenneby-Helleday, Clara","Jacobsson, Hans","Linder, Stig","Misra, Sougat","Bjornstedt, Mikael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-326262"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska University Hospital"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pharmacokinetics and toxicity of sodium selenite in the treatment of patients with carcinoma in a phase i clinical trial the secar study","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/113381938/nutrients_07_04978.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-326262","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4488827","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84929347750","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26102212","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-48f6b249-7883-42b3-a01c-81948c1867b8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EDD5FF6DE3C4B17AA3D88342AA47C15C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/113381938/nutrients_07_04978.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu7064978","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4488827/","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"karolinska university hospital","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Ka