k":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"brodersenpau","last":"Brodersen","locid":"ba6cc461-7190-47ac-8257-f3c4ec9e415b","name":"Brodersen, Anna Haugaard","name_org":"Brodersen, Anna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte","key":"bjerrepau","last":"Bjerre","name":"Bjerre, Charlotte","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"85","place":"København","publisher":"Professionshøjskolen Metropol","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","KP","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kp-1bb87fa8-d29c-4c30-b692-6cd38c1e2a25","kp-972b6cd2-e3c7-4bdf-b6b8-3c6570ae9b6e"],"src":["KP University College Copenhagen"],"title":"Udsatte unge på erhvervsuddannelserne : – imellem inklusion, marginalisering og fragmentering?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.75-038567","year":"2015","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To describe establishment and organisation of the Danish Fetal Medicine Database and to report national results of first trimester combined screening for trisomy 21 in the five year period 2008-2012. DESIGN: National register study using prospectively collected first trimester screening data from the Danish Fetal Medicine Database POPULATION: Pregnant women in Denmark undergoing first trimester screening for trisomy 21. METHODS: Data on maternal characteristics, biochemical and ultrasonic markers are continuously sent electronically from local fetal medicine databases (Astraia Gmbh software) to a central national database. Data are linked to outcome data from the National Birth Register, the National Patient Register and the National Cytogenetic Register via the mother's unique personal registration number. First trimester screening data from 2008-2012 were retrieved. Main outcome measures Screening performance was assessed for the years 2008-2012 by calculating detection rates and screen-positive rates RESULTS: A total of 268 342 first trimester risk assessments for trisomy 21 were performed in singleton pregnancies. Participation rate in first trimester screening was >90%. The national screen-positive rate increased from 3.6% in 2008 to 4.7% in 2012. The national detection rate of trisomy 21 was reported to be between 82-90% in the 5-year period CONCLUSION: A national fetal medicine database has been successfully established in Denmark. Results from the database have shown that at a national level first trimester screening performance for trisomy 21 is high with a low screen-positive rate and a high detection rate. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.","author_count":18,"contributors":["Ekelund, Charlotte K","Petersen, Olav B","Jørgensen, Finn S","Kjaergaard, Susanne","Larsen, Torben","Olesen, Annette W","Skibsted, Lillian","Skovbo, Peter","Sommer, Steffen","Sperling, Lene","Stavnstrup, Benedicte","Størup, Birgitte",